WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.300
Before planning a migration, teams need to understand what cryptography is doing in each place.

2
00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:13.378
Key establishment is about helping two parties arrive at shared secret material over an untrusted network.

3
00:00:13.378 --> 00:00:17.967
A key encapsulation mechanism, or KEM, is one way to support that role.

4
00:00:17.967 --> 00:00:31.267
Conceptually, one party creates protected key material that another party can recover with the right private information, allowing both sides to derive a shared secret for later symmetric encryption.

5
00:00:31.267 --> 00:00:34.067
Digital signatures play a different role.

6
00:00:34.067 --> 00:00:42.467
A signature lets a verifier check that data was signed by the holder of a private key and that the signed data has not been modified.

7
00:00:42.467 --> 00:00:54.912
That supports authentication, integrity, software update trust, code signing, certificate authorities, secure boot chains, identity tokens, document signing, and device enrollment.

8
00:00:54.912 --> 00:00:58.878
Signatures do not automatically provide confidentiality.

9
00:00:58.878 --> 00:01:03.078
They answer questions about origin and integrity, not secrecy.

10
00:01:03.078 --> 00:01:07.590
These roles appear in many systems that practitioners use every day.

11
00:01:07.590 --> 00:01:12.878
TLS uses key establishment and authentication to protect web sessions and APIs.

12
00:01:12.878 --> 00:01:16.690
VPNs and SSH use public-key mechanisms for remote access.

13
00:01:16.690 --> 00:01:20.034
Software updates and packages rely on signatures.

14
00:01:20.034 --> 00:01:22.601
Certificates bind keys to identities.

15
00:01:22.601 --> 00:01:33.101
Secure email, device authentication, identity platforms, and firmware signing all depend on cryptographic roles that may have to change at different speeds.

16
00:01:33.101 --> 00:01:38.623
Replacing one algorithm is therefore rarely a simple find-and-replace operation.

17
00:01:38.623 --> 00:01:44.923
A KEM change can affect protocol negotiation, message size, performance, and library support.

18
00:01:44.923 --> 00:01:54.412
A signature change can affect certificate formats, chain sizes, hardware security module behavior, storage, logging, and verification code.

19
00:01:54.412 --> 00:01:59.779
The lesson is practical: identify the role first, then choose the migration path.

20
00:01:59.779 --> 00:02:06.857
A team that treats all cryptography as one category will miss dependencies and create avoidable surprises.
